When I first thought about this project, I was not planning to build an experimentation framework.

The original idea was much smaller.

I was teaching myself Reinforcement Learning and kept wondering how I could connect the concepts I was learning to something interactive. Games seemed like a natural place to start.

One random thought eventually became:

> What if I built an agent that could play a Guess Who?-style game?

At first, I only wanted to get the mechanics working.

Could I generate a set of characters?

Could an agent ask yes/no questions?

Could it eliminate candidates after receiving an answer?

Could it eventually guess the correct character?

That was the entire idea.

But as I continued building, debugging, comparing strategies, and introducing RL, the project gradually became something much larger.

Looking back, this was probably the point where I realized the project had become much more than an RL exercise.

It had become a place where I could change the conditions of a decision-making problem and observe what happened.

That is also why the name RL Playground started making more sense.

The guessing game was no longer just a game.

It was an experiment.

From One Run to Repeated Experiments

Watching a single game can be interesting as it tells me something about one trajectory.

But one trajectory does not tell me very much about an algorithm.

Maybe the target character was unusually easy to isolate.

Maybe the first question happened to produce an extremely favourable split.

Maybe randomness caused the agent to make an early guess.

To understand how a strategy actually behaves, I needed repeated experiments.

This led to the simulation system.

Instead of asking:

What happened in this game?

I could ask:

What happens over 100 games?

or:

What happens when I increase the search space?

or:

What happens when I introduce more randomness?

or:

Does this strategy remain effective when the environment becomes more difficult?

The simulation engine allowed experiments to run across multiple epochs.

This changed the scale of what I could study.

Comparing Different Ways of Making Decisions

One of the most important parts of the system was building multiple agents.

The project eventually included several different approaches to the same problem.

Random Agent

The simplest baseline.

The agent does not try to intelligently identify useful questions.

Its purpose is not to perform well.

Its purpose is to answer an important experimental question:

How much better is an intelligent strategy than essentially random decision-making?

Without a weak baseline, it is difficult to understand whether a more advanced algorithm is actually doing something meaningful.

Cardinality Entropy

This strategy evaluates the structure of the search space and uses distribution-based reasoning to choose questions.

The goal is to reduce the candidate pool efficiently.

This represented one of my earliest attempts to move beyond random gameplay and give the agent a deliberate strategy.

Binary Information Gain

This became a stronger information-theoretic baseline.

Instead of simply thinking about which value appeared frequently, the strategy focused more directly on how well a question could divide the remaining search space.

The ideal question is close to:

50% Yes
50% No

because neither possible answer leaves the agent with an overwhelmingly large candidate pool.

This is closely related to the theoretical structure of binary search.

This gave me a useful reference point.

Epsilon-Information Gain

I also wanted to introduce controlled randomness.

A purely deterministic heuristic always follows its calculated best action.

But real decision processes can involve exploration, mistakes, noise, or deliberate deviation from the current best option.

The epsilon-style strategy allowed me to experiment with this balance.

Sometimes:

Follow the best information-based action

Other times:

Explore another option

This created another interesting question:

Does introducing randomness always make a strategy worse, or can controlled exploration sometimes change behaviour in useful ways?

Deep Q-Network

Finally, the RL agent represented a fundamentally different approach.

The heuristic agents already contain mathematical reasoning about what makes a useful question.

The DQN does not begin with that rule.

Instead, it receives:

a state representation;
available interactions with the environment;
rewards;
and repeated experience.

Its job is to learn a policy from the consequences of its actions.

This made the comparison much more interesting to me.

The experiment was no longer simply:

Algorithm A vs Algorithm B

It became a comparison between different philosophies of decision-making all solving the same underlying search problem.

Introducing Controlled Imperfection

Another important part of the experimentation system was the ability to change the override_probability.

This came from an earlier idea in the project.

I originally introduced probabilistic guessing to simulate imperfect behaviour.

At any turn, an agent could potentially override its normal decision process and make a direct guess.

At first, this was mainly part of the gameplay logic.

Later, it became an experimental variable.

I could ask:

What happens when I increase the probability of overriding a rational strategy?

For example:

Override Probability = 0.00

The strategy behaves deterministically.

Override Probability = 0.10

The agent occasionally deviates.

Override Probability = 0.30

The decision process becomes substantially more stochastic.

This allows the environment to test robustness.

A strong strategy should not only look good under perfect conditions.

It is interesting to see what happens when its behaviour becomes noisy.

How quickly does performance degrade?

Does the number of steps increase?

Does the win rate collapse immediately?

Or does the strategy remain relatively stable?

These were exactly the kinds of questions I had not imagined asking when I first started writing the game logic.

Scaling the Search Space

One of the most interesting things I noticed while working on the project was how behaviour changed as the search space grew.

The first versions of the game used relatively simple feature spaces.

That was intentional.

I wanted to make sure the mechanics worked before introducing more complexity.

As the number of possible characters increased, the environment changed significantly.

A strategy that looked effective in a small search space did not necessarily behave the same way in a larger one.

This gave the project another experimental dimension:

Small Search Space
        ↓
Medium Search Space
        ↓
Large Search Space
        ↓
High-Cardinality Search Problem

Now I could observe:

how the average number of steps scaled;
whether win rates remained stable;
which heuristics degraded first;
whether the RL policy generalized;
and how close different strategies came to the theoretical information limit.

This was one of the things that made the project feel more substantial than a simple game implementation.

The game mechanics stayed familiar.

But by increasing the number and structure of possible states, I could turn it into a much larger sequential decision problem.

Measuring More Than Wins

At first, success seemed simple.

Did the agent guess the correct character?

Yes → Win

No → Loss

But that quickly became too limited.

Two agents could both have:

100% win rate

while behaving very differently.

For example:

Agent A:
Average steps = 12

versus:

Agent B:
Average steps = 45

Technically, both solve the game.

But they do not solve it with the same efficiency.

This is why the experiment needed multiple metrics.

Win Rate as the most obvious measure.

This answers:

Can the strategy reliably solve the task?

Number of Steps

This measures how efficiently the strategy reaches a conclusion.

A lower number is not automatically better in every situation.

An agent could guess immediately and occasionally get lucky.

So this metric needs to be interpreted alongside win rate.

Together, they answer:

How often does the strategy succeed, and how much interaction does success require?

Search-Space Contraction

This became one of the most interesting metrics for me.

Instead of looking only at the final outcome, I could observe the trajectory of the search process itself.

For example:

Step 0 → 4,200 candidates
Step 1 → 2,100
Step 2 → 1,050
Step 3 → 520
Step 4 → 260

This produces a very different pattern from:

Step 0 → 4,200 candidates
Step 1 → 4,000
Step 2 → 3,600
Step 3 → 3,100
Step 4 → 2,900

Both agents are technically making progress.

But the structure of that progress is completely different.

Search-space contraction allowed me to see how an algorithm thinks over time, at least through the consequences of its decisions.

That was much more interesting than only looking at whether the final guess was correct.

The Plots Became Part of the Experiment

Visualization was not originally a major part of the idea.

At first, terminal output was enough.

Then the project grew.

Once I started running multiple simulations and comparing multiple agents, raw numbers became much harder to interpret.

The plots gave me another way to study the experiment.

I could visualize:

win rates across simulations;
average steps;
candidate-pool reduction;
search-space contraction curves;
the effect of override probability;
and differences between agents.

The most important change was that I could now see patterns rather than just calculate them.

The experiment became visual.

And for me, that made the behaviour of the algorithms much easier to reason about.

From a Game to a Playground

This was the point where I started seeing the larger potential of the project.

The Guessing Game was built around a specific problem:

Sequentially reduce uncertainty until a hidden state can be identified.

But the framework behind it could support many different experiments.

The core pattern is:

Environment has hidden state
          │
          ▼
Agent receives observations
          │
          ▼
Agent selects an action
          │
          ▼
Environment transitions
          │
          ▼
Agent receives feedback
          │
          ▼
Repeat

That pattern appears everywhere in Reinforcement Learning.

The guessing game gave me one concrete environment where I could experiment with:

state representation;
action-space design;
exploration;
reward shaping;
stochasticity;
policy learning;
baseline comparison;
and evaluation.

I do not want this repository to become a collection of finished tutorial implementations.

I want it to become a record of experimentation.

A place where I can take something I am learning, build an environment around it, test my assumptions, make mistakes, and document what actually happens.

What the Project Can Be Used For

The current Guessing Game can already be used in several different ways.

1. Comparing Decision-Making Strategies

Different agents can be placed inside the same environment and evaluated under identical conditions.

This makes it possible to compare:

random decision-making;
deterministic heuristics;
stochastic heuristics;
information-theoretic approaches;
and learned RL policies.

The environment provides a controlled setting where the decision problem stays constant while the strategy changes.

2. Studying Search-Space Complexity

The character generator can create larger or more complex candidate spaces.

This makes it possible to experiment with questions such as:

How does performance scale as the search problem grows?

Which strategies are robust to increasing complexity?

Does an RL policy trained on one search-space size generalize to another?

3. Studying Noise and Imperfect Decisions

The override mechanism allows controlled stochasticity.

Instead of assuming that an agent always follows its ideal policy, the experiment can introduce deviations and observe their effects.

This can be useful for studying:

robustness;
sensitivity to randomness;
exploration versus exploitation;
and performance degradation.
4. Experimenting With RL Design

Perhaps the most important use for me personally is that the environment gives me a place to test RL design decisions.

I can change:

the observation;
the action representation;
the reward function;
the episode length;
the exploration behaviour;
or the learning algorithm.

Then I can observe what changes.

The environment becomes a practical way to ask:

What happens if I design this differently?

That is much more valuable to me than simply implementing an algorithm from a notebook.

5. Education and Documentation

The project can also serve as a learning resource.

Not because it presents a perfect solution.

In fact, the opposite is more interesting.

The documentation records:

the original assumptions;
failed approaches;
bugs;
redesigns;
and the reasoning behind changes.

That progression is probably more representative of how I actually learned than a polished final implementation alone would be.

Looking Back at the Original Idea

I started this because I was self-learning Reinforcement Learning.

I did not want to only read equations and complete tutorials.

I wanted to find a way to make the concepts interact with something real.

The original question was almost casual:

Could I make an agent play this game?

Somewhere along the way, I ended up dealing with:

combinatorial dataset generation;
search algorithms;
entropy;
information gain;
state representations;
high-dimensional action spaces;
exploration failure;
action constraints;
reward shaping;
episode truncation;
DQN training;
Gymnasium environments;
repeated simulations;
metrics;
and visualization.

The project grew because every answer created another question.

That was not something I planned.

It happened naturally through experimentation.

And I think that is what I want the RL Playground to continue being.

Not a repository where every project begins with:

Here is the algorithm I am going to implement.

But a place where projects can begin with:

I wonder what would happen if I tried this?

The Real Outcome So Far

The RL agent is one outcome of this project.

The heuristic benchmarks are another.

The simulation dashboard is another.

But I think the biggest outcome is the environment itself.

I now have a system where I can change variables, introduce different agents, run repeated experiments, and observe how strategies behave under changing conditions.

And that gives the project room to keep growing.

I can add:

new agents;
new RL algorithms;
different reward functions;
new state representations;
more complex character constraints;
curriculum learning experiments;
generalization tests;
multi-agent variations;
and entirely different deduction environments.

The project started as one small experiment.

Now it feels more like a foundation.

Where the Playground Goes Next

The Guessing Game is currently the first major environment inside the RL Playground.

It will probably not be the last.

I also want to use the repository to work through ideas and experiments from my Reinforcement Learning studies, including concepts inspired by Sutton and Barto.

Some experiments may be small.

Some may fail.

Some may only exist to answer one question I had while learning.

That is part of the point.

The goal is not for every experiment to become a finished application.

The goal is to build, test, observe, and understand.

This project taught me that there is a big difference between reading that an algorithm behaves in a certain way and actually building an environment where you can watch that behaviour emerge.

The Guessing Game gave me my first playground for doing that.

And it has already grown far beyond the random idea that started it.